WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.020
Sodium conductance turns on fast, then shuts itself off.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.080
Potassium turns on slowly and stays on.

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:11.960
What channel does that?

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:20.003
It takes three processes: fast sodium activation, slower sodium inactivation, and

5
00:00:20.003 --> 00:00:21.523
slow potassium activation.

6
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:24.023
Picture them as gates.

7
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:28.703
A sodium channel has two gates in a row, like two doors in one hallway.

8
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.833
The fast gate, for activation, is shut at rest and snaps open on depolarization.

9
00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:40.623
The slow gate, for inactivation, is open at rest and shuts several times more

10
00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:41.623
slowly.

11
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:47.087
Sodium passes only while both are open: a brief window before the slow gate shuts.

12
00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:52.597
The squid's delayed potassium channel behaves as if it has one slow gate.

13
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.870
This is a cartoon.

14
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:04.430
The 1952 equations track the three processes with variables called m, h and n.

15
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:08.653
Their powers were fitted to the data, not counted from gates.

16
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:11.403
Now let the axon run free.

17
00:01:12.303 --> 00:01:14.143
A ghost marker shows the balance point.

18
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:16.473
Moment one, the rise.

19
00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:21.993
Activation gates open, sodium flows in, and the inside gets more positive.

20
00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:23.993
That opens more gates.

21
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:26.487
This is positive feedback.

22
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.067
Sodium conductance soon dwarfs potassium's.

23
00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:34.567
The balance point leaps toward plus +50 and the voltage races after it.

24
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.410
Even during the rise, the slow processes begin.

25
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.230
Inactivation is already developing, and potassium gates start to open.

26
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.370
Here is a question.

27
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.770
Why does the spike stop short of plus +50 mV?

28
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.130
The voltage stops where it meets the balance point.

29
00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.090
There, inward sodium current equals outward potassium and leak current.

30
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:08.823
Sodium still has about 12 times potassium's conductance.

31
00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:12.523
But its pull is weak, because the voltage is near its end.

32
00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:18.567
Potassium and leak keep the balance point below plus +50 During the rise,

33
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:23.167
inactivation lowers sodium conductance, and potassium conductance climbs.

34
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.890
In the model, the risen potassium conductance matters most.

35
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.070
The peak is about plus +40

36
00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.473
Moment three, the fall.

37
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:38.273
Sodium conductance collapses as inactivation closes channels, while potassium

38
00:02:38.273 --> 00:02:39.573
conductance keeps rising.

39
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:44.183
The balance point drops below the voltage, so the voltage falls.

40
00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:48.043
At first, sodium may still have the larger conductance.

41
00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:53.843
Soon potassium becomes the largest, and the balance point sinks toward potassium's

42
00:02:53.843 --> 00:02:54.843
end.

43
00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:56.573
This is negative feedback.

44
00:02:57.493 --> 00:03:01.493
The same depolarization that started the spike also ends it, a little later.

45
00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:04.237
Moment four, the undershoot.

46
00:03:05.037 --> 00:03:08.857
Potassium conductance stays above rest, because its gates close slowly.

47
00:03:09.737 --> 00:03:12.737
The balance point and voltage dip toward minus −77

48
00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:16.277
Fast sodium activation makes the rise.

49
00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:20.397
Slower inactivation and potassium activation end it.
